y great relationships  with the FBI they are sort of back and  forth relationships Hoover legitimated a  lot of their interests often helped  these organizations out and when he was  in trouble they often came to put  pressure on congressman and to write  letters to newspapers in defense of j  edgar Hoover so I think we have to think  about him as a political organizer as  well my last thing that I will say about  j edgar Hoover as a bureaucrat is that I  have become convinced that the single  most important thing that he did in his  entire career in order to maintain power  was not in fact you know knowing about  john f kennedy's affairs or following  eleanor roosevelt and going to franklin  and reporting about who she in fact was  seeing the single most important thing  he did to main power was to keep his  agents out of the civil service system  he fought his entire career to keep his  agents out of civil service and what  this meant was that he was actually able  to build an agency in which he himself  had almost sole control over the hiring  the firing of agents the kind of person  he wanted he justified this not on the  grounds of personal control but on the  grounds of saying that the FBI is so  fabulous and its standards are so high  that they simply cannot take the sort of  mass of people who are being recommended  out of the civil service but again these  kinds of bureaucratic questions these  kinds of State Building questions I  think are as important for understanding  J Edgar Hoover as what files he might  have maintained so that's sort of the  first piece I said so the driving force  at least in part of this biography is  how does an ideological conservative  maintain state power throughout the age  of American liberalism so we've got a  little bit of the power story there I  want to quickly talk about the  literature on conservatism and just  suggest a few ways in which i think  Hoover tells us some new and interesting  things there  so again over the