nd raises really  interesting questions about how we want  think about the role the conservatism  has played in modern American politics  so Hoover in 1972 is looking back to  nineteen nineteen and saying we're  seeing the same thing we're seeing these  kinds of bombings we're seeing disorder  the same things that Richard Nixon is  saying in that little clip that I showed  j edgar Hoover is already beginning to  use that language in the teens in the  20s and in the 30s so I think his story  suggests that we want to kind of break  apart this story that modern  conservatism comes out of the reaction  to the New Deal and out of the 1950s the  second thing that I think Hoover is  useful for thinking about is that as I  said a lot of the ways in which we tend  to think about conservative politics is  either in an electoral frame or in a  kind of social movement frame right so  you've got the grassroots story this  kind of resurgence that begins in the  50s into the 60s and 70s or you have a  kind of electoral politics story well  Hoover is a very interesting figure in  this regard as I said he's actually  someone who is not sort of operating  outside the Centers of Washington power  he is deeply embedded in them throughout  this whole period and I think it's  interesting to consider and i'll be  happy to expand upon this in the Q&amp;A a  little bit the interesting to consider  Hoover has a kind of conservative state  builder so in what ways do his  conservative politics shape what he does  with in his agency and in what ways do  his conservative politics sort of shaped  the kind of message that he is sending  out into the world on the third piece  that I'll mention quickly is that I  think it's very important to think about  the FBI within the context of this story  of I don't know what kind of battle  between the left and the right  throughout these years or between  liberalism and conservatism because the  fact of j edgar hoover and the kinds of  politics that he had actually meant that  people on the ground activists on the  ground had very very different  relationships to state power it is  fundamentally different experience to be  organizing on the left and we all  probably basically know the story of the  FBI's relationship  with the new left with the Black  Panthers with the anti-war movement  right it's a fundamentally different  experience to fear that your  organization is being infiltrated to  know that the federal government is  watching you and is against you than it  is to be organizing even in a similar  grassroots organization on the right and  know that in some sense the FBI is  behind you that j edgar Hoover is  basically tolerant or supportive of your  politics now I don't think this explains  everything certainly about what's going  on in social movements but to the degree  that we are talking in the 1960s now  about a kind of battle that's emerged  between the left and the right during  those years I think it's important to  see that their relationships with the  state and with the FBI in particular are  not equivalent and that they're very  different on the ground experiences in  part because of this man who is there at  the top in the FBI all right just to  finish up I want to say a few things  going back to our our nixon at Jagger  Hoover's funeral I said you know the  things that stood out to me about that  clip were number one the fact of the  funeral right the fact that this  unelected bureaucrat was able to bring  together the kind of power and interest  and cultural influence that Hoover did  in 1972 and in the years leading up to  that um the second was that he stood for  something right he stood for a  particular kind of conservative politics  and really was a bulwark of that kind of  conservative politics throughout this  period we describe as the age of  American liberalism the third piece that  I just want to say that I mentioned at  the beginning and want to expand upon  very briefly is just how much Hoover's  image has changed since that moment now  certainly there were lots of people who  hated J Edgar Hoover throughout his life  but I think there's something to be  explained in just how dramatically his  image has changed over the last 40 years  and probably the best embodiment of this  for me is that in 1975 this is three  years after he died the FBI finally got  its own building on pence  vania Avenue it had been within the  Justice Department's building previous  to that and when the FBI got its own  building it was labeled the J Edgar  Hoover Building was put up as this kind  of great monument to the great man who  had built the bureau today they are  actually probably going to knock down  that building because it's kind of a  monstrosity you know like mid 70s  terrible architecture looks kind of like  the old New Haven Coliseum so you can  see so they are probably going to move  out of this building and there is an  almost universal assumption that when  they move out of that building and when  they knock that building down you know  the next FBI building is not going to be  called the J Edgar Hoover Building and  in fact there have been battles over the  last 15 to 20 years to actually have his  name stripped off of the FBI building  that is still standing there so I think  on the one hand this is the product of  what I was saying earlier right we do  know more about him right we've had  these expose A's we know more about the  lawlessness and the corruption and the  real venality that the FBI was engaged  in over that time but I think it's  important to store size our own thoughts  as well so I'll just leave you with a  couple of reasons why i think j edgar  Hoover's image has changed so much going  beyond the fact that and we just know  some more of the really problematic  things that were going on the first is  that Hoover happened to die at a moment  of expose right he happened to die as  Watergate is beginning to take off and  this really comes to fruition with  things like the Church Committee and  with other expose A's of the CIA of  other intelligence agencies and new  constraints on intelligence agencies and  in that context I do think probably  deservedly so Hoover emerged as  something of a scapegoat as a person  upon whom you could say oh look at all  of those terrible terrible things that  happened well there's the man who did  them and now he's dead and he was a very  very bad man and none of the rest of us  actually have to take this too seriously  or think about taking responsibility for  it ourselves so I think it is both  served you know truth but has also had  the  effect of kind of absolving other people  of a certain kind of responsibility and  limiting the degree to which we're going  to think in broader terms it was just  that one bad at that very powerful Apple  that bad apple I think the second thing  that's worth thinking about is why  people have become increasingly  interested in Hoover's sex life and you  know I mean it's inherently pretty  interesting on the one hand on the other  hand it is actually I think also a  product of a pretty profound shift  that's happened in how we think about  our public officials and I think to some  degree for the better and to some degree  for the worse in the sense that you know  not only do we not know a lot about j  edgar Hoover's sex life at the time but  we didn't know a lot about what a whole  lot of other powerful people were doing  in their intimate relationships and so  on the one hand it has this kind of  scandalous and interesting quality to it  on the other hand I think we do have to  think about Hoover in relationship to a  broader culture which has begun to take  seriously I think for good reasons and  for bad the the personal lives of its  public figures and how we want to put  those pieces together so I see that I'm  actually I run a little bit over my  prescribed time which was 40 minutes so  i think i will actually just leave it  there for now but last thing that I want  to say is that overall one of the  challenges of writing about Hoover is  that I am trying to write a biography  that takes him seriously and really  looks at his power and his influence  takes his personal life as childhood  seriously at the same time without  writing a redemptive biography of a man  who I think in many ways I had a very  very destructive influence on the United  States and on the shape of its political  culture and on the shape of American  government so I will leave it there for  now hopefully we can do some questions  you

